<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/7th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Nineveh (627)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/627">627 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-sasanian-war-of-602-628">Byzantine–Sasanian War of 602–628</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> was fought in 627 CE near the ruins of ancient Nineveh in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, where the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> defeated a Sasanian army. It was the climax of the Byzantine-Sasanian war of 602–628. The defeat led to the overthrow of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a> and a succession crisis that left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> exhausted on the eve of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Arab conquests</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After Emperor Maurice was murdered by the usurper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocas">Phocas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a> declared war under the pretext of avenging his benefactor. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> conquered much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and even some of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, but the resurgence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> forced them onto the defensive. Allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a>, they failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. During that siege Heraclius allied himself with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-turkic-khaganate">Western Turkic Khaganate</a> under Tong Yabghu, whom Byzantine sources call the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> under Ziebel. The Turks sent 40,000 men into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> in 626, and joint operations focused on besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In mid-September 627 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> left Ziebel at the siege and invaded the Persian Empire with between 25,000 and 50,000 troops and 40,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürks</a>. The Göktürks soon deserted him because of the strange winter conditions. Khosrow appointed Rhahzadh to confront him, with an army of 12,000. Heraclius evaded him, entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and took food and fodder from the countryside, leaving Rhahzadh to follow through stripped land. On 1 December he crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zab">Great Zab</a> and camped near the ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> chose a plain west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zab">Great Zab</a> where his lances and hand-to-hand fighting counted for most, and fog reduced the Persian advantage in missile troops. On 12 December Rhahzadh attacked in three masses. Heraclius <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feigned-retreat">feigned retreat</a> to draw them onto the plain, then turned on them. After eight hours the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> withdrew to the foothills, with 6,000 dead. Nikephoros says Rhahzadh challenged Heraclius to single combat and was killed by him with one thrust, and Theophanes supports this, though doubt has been cast on it. 3,000 Persian reinforcements arrived too late.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> could not capture the Persian camp, but no Persian army remained to oppose them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> plundered Khosrow's palace at Dastagird and recovered 300 captured standards. He could not attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a> because the Nahrawan Canal was blocked by a fallen bridge. The Persian army rebelled and overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a>, raising his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kavad-ii">Kavad II</a>, also called Siroes, and Khosrow died in a dungeon, shot slowly with arrows on the fifth day.</p><p>Kavad at once sent peace offers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a>, knowing that his own empire was near exhaustion, imposed no harsh terms. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> regained all their lost territories, their captured soldiers, a war indemnity and, of great spiritual significance, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a> and other relics lost at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 614. It was the last conflict of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-persian-wars">Roman–Persian Wars</a>. The Sasanian civil war that followed weakened the empire and contributed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-conquest-of-persia">Muslim conquest of Persia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Nineveh (627)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Heraclius's counter-offensive from 622 CE reversed these gains within six years, culminating in the <b>Battle of Nineveh</b> on 12 December 627 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
